so today was the first day of three days  of oral arguments over the Patient  Protection and Affordable Care Act and  today was in some sense the driest of  the days it's the first day it's sort of  the warm up and this was about whether  or not the anti-injunction act which is  this tax statute dating back to 1867  which says that you cannot challenge  basically cannot challenge a federal tax  in federal court until the tax has been  assessed and paid and so if the  individual mandate which requires all  Americans to fire secure health  insurance or else you pay a penalty is  that if that penalty is a tax then the  legal challenge cannot proceed and so  that's what the court was addressing  today is this in fact a tax and does  that mean that they're the challengers  have to wait until 2015 a big takeaway  from me today was that a majority of the  justices seemed unwilling to allow the  anti-injunction Act to get in the way of  the broader legal challenge there were  it with weather was Justice Breyer  pointing out repeatedly that well you  know Congress called it penalty not  attacks and that that basically you know  just because it sounds like a tax  doesn't make it attacks you had other  justices making similar in the solar  comments justice Sotomayor said  something to the effect of was in kind  of the problem in artful drafting by  Congress which I think we all agree is  correct rate perhaps across the whole  you know scope of this bill so i don't i  don't expect that this is going to be  the issue that derails the legal  challenge I think the justices the  government everyone's in agreement that  they want a real ruling on the  constitutional merits not on this more  arcane tax issue
 

